---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Subscribe from "../components/Subscribe.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`How ${SITE.title} is produced: sources, drafting, checking and approval.`}
>
  <article class="article prose">
    <h1 class="article__title">About {SITE.title}</h1>

    <p>
      {SITE.title} publishes one short article a day about what the tech and AI world is talking
      about. Every article is machine-drafted from sources that are listed at the bottom of the
      page, and every one is approved by a person before it goes out.
    </p>

    <h2>How an article gets made</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Collect.</strong> Each morning the pipeline pulls recent items from Hacker News,
        arXiv, GitHub and Reddit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cluster.</strong> Items are embedded and grouped by meaning, so several sources
        talking about the same thing become one topic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Score.</strong> Topics are ranked by velocity, novelty, how many items they have,
        how many sources back them, and engagement measured within each source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Draft.</strong> A language model writes a 400 to 600 word article using those
        sources only, and quotes each one with its URL.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check.</strong> The draft is rejected unless it meets length limits, cites only the
        supplied URLs, avoids placeholder text and uses valid tags. A rejected draft gets one
        rewrite, and if it fails again nothing is published that day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Approve and publish.</strong> The draft is sent to a person with the full text and
        its source list. It is published only after they approve it.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What AI assistance means here</h2>
    <p>
      The prose is written by a model, so treat it as a starting point rather than a finished
      reference. The model is told to use only the supplied sources, not to invent numbers, dates,
      quotes or version numbers, and to name the source behind each claim. The checker also rejects
      any URL in the body that was not in the source list.
    </p>
    <p>
      The source list itself is built by code, not by the model, and each entry links to the
      original item. If a claim matters to you, open the source.
    </p>

    <h2>A person approves every article</h2>
    <p>
      Drafts are not published automatically. Each one waits in a private approval queue with its
      full text, and a human decides whether it goes out. Quiet days are skipped instead of padded,
      and a topic that ran recently is left alone unless its activity has roughly doubled.
    </p>

    <h2>What it will not do</h2>
    <p>
      It does not publish filler on thin days, it does not repeat a topic inside its cooldown
      window, and it does not post the same article twice.
    </p>
  </article>

  <Subscribe />
</BaseLayout>
